course: dictionary
lesson: 14690
title: Prevail: win, common, or persuade
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prevail has three meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. The first is to win in the end after a struggle. «After a long fight, the kind knight prevailed over the dragon.» A fight is a struggle, so prevailed must mean won in the end.
explain: The second meaning is to be common or usual in a place or time. «In our town, fog prevails on cold winter mornings.» The words cold winter mornings tell you that fog is usual then, so prevails means is common there.
explain: The third meaning is formal. Prevail on or prevail upon means to persuade someone to do something. «I prevailed upon Dad to let me stay up for the film.» Persuaded is a good swap here, because it fits the sentence.
explain: Always try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word. If won, was common, or persuaded makes sense, you have the right meaning. If none of them fits, read the sentence again and look for clues.
fact: Prevail means to win in the end after a struggle, or to be common in a place or time.
fact: A prevailing wind is the wind that blows most often in a place, as in geography lessons.
fact: Prevail upon someone is a formal phrase that means to persuade them to do something.
fact: «Common sense prevailed» means that sensible thinking won out in the end.
fact: A good check is to swap in won, common, or persuaded and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «After a long struggle, the good knight prevailed.» What does prevailed mean here? | Fell asleep | Lost his sword | Ran away from the battle | *Won in the end
exercise: Geography: «The prevailing wind in our town blows from the west.» What does prevailing wind mean? | *The wind that blows most often | A wind that makes no sound | The strongest wind ever recorded | The coldest wind of the winter
exercise: Mum says, «I managed to prevail upon the lady to lend us her car.» What does prevail upon mean? | Hide from someone | *Persuade someone to do something | Argue loudly with someone | Give someone a present
test: «Silly rules prevailed in the old school for many years.» What does prevailed mean here? | Was forgotten | Was unhappy | Was broken | *Was common and usual
test: «Justice prevailed, and the thief gave the money back.» What does justice prevailed mean? | *The right side won in the end | Justice was very slow | The thief got away | Nobody cared about the money
exam: «Common sense prevailed when the children chose to stay indoors in the storm.» What does prevailed mean here? | Became lost in the storm | Was never heard of | Was an old, rare word | *Won out over other ideas
